  "recovery_stream_end": null,
  "recovery_latency_s": 129.5925576686859,
  "client_view_text": " When a database crash occurs, the write-ahead log helps the database recover its data by replaying all the transactions that were written to the log but not yet committed to the database. Here\u2019s how it works: when a database executes a transaction, it writes the transaction to the log first, before writing it to the database. This ensures that all transactions are written to the log in the same order in which they were executed. When the database crashes, the log provides a record of all the transactions that were in progress at the time of the crash. By replaying these transactions in the order they were written to the log, the database can recover its data to the point just before the crash. This ensures that any data written by the transactions that were in progress at the time of the crash is included in the recovered database, while any data written by transactions that were not yet committed is discarded.\n\nA write-ahead log allows the database to recover from a crash by replaying the transactions that were written to the log but not yet committed to the database. Here\u2019s how it works:\n\n1.  All transactions are written to the log before they are written to the database.\n2.  The log provides a record of all the transactions that were in progress at the time of the crash"
}